sections.s2_technical:
Let me take each limitation in turn, the way I would when a team outgrows the default scheduler.

Limitation one — SINGLE-THREADED. By default Spring creates ONE thread for all `@Scheduled` tasks. If you have three jobs and one takes an hour, the other two cannot run until it finishes, even though their trigger time arrived. This silently serialises everything. The fix is to give the scheduler a pool. I implement `SchedulingConfigurer` and register a `ThreadPoolTaskScheduler` with several threads, or set `spring.task.scheduling.pool.size`. Now independent jobs run concurrently.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableScheduling
public class SchedulerConfig implements SchedulingConfigurer {
    @Override public void configureTasks(ScheduledTaskRegistrar registrar) {
        ThreadPoolTaskScheduler pool = new ThreadPoolTaskScheduler();
        pool.setPoolSize(5);                 // multiple scheduled jobs run concurrently
        pool.setThreadNamePrefix("sched-");
        pool.initialize();
        registrar.setTaskScheduler(pool);
    }
}
```

A related tactic: keep the `@Scheduled` method itself quick and offload the heavy work to an `@Async` method on a separate executor, so the scheduler thread is freed immediately. But note a single slow cron still ties up one pool thread, so sizing the pool to the number of concurrent jobs matters.

Limitation two — NO PERSISTENCE. `@Scheduled` triggers exist only in the running JVM's memory. There is no stored job definition, no execution history, and no catch-up. If the application is down (deploy, crash, maintenance) exactly when a cron should fire, that run is simply MISSED and never happens — nobody records it and nothing runs it late. For a best-effort cache refresh that is fine; for a regulatory report that MUST run, it is a serious gap. The fix is a persistent scheduler: Quartz with a `JDBCJobStore` stores jobs and triggers in the database, survives restarts, and provides MISFIRE handling — policies that decide whether a trigger missed while the app was down should fire immediately, fire once, or be skipped. Alternatively an external scheduler (a Kubernetes CronJob, cron, or an enterprise scheduler) invokes an endpoint, decoupling the schedule from the app lifecycle.

Limitation three — NO RUNTIME RESCHEDULING. The cron string in `@Scheduled(cron = "...")` is bound at startup. You cannot change the schedule while the app runs — to change it you redeploy. Business often needs to adjust a job's time without a release (move the statement run earlier at month-end, pause a job during an incident). The fix is PROGRAMMATIC scheduling: inject a `TaskScheduler` and schedule a `Runnable` with a `Trigger` whose next execution is computed from a cron value READ DYNAMICALLY (from config, database, or a feature flag). Because the trigger re-reads the cron each time it computes the next fire, changing the stored value reschedules the job live. Quartz offers the same via `rescheduleJob`.

```java
// Dynamic cron: the Trigger re-reads the cron value on each computation → runtime reschedule
taskScheduler.schedule(this::runJob, triggerContext -> {
    String cron = cronProvider.currentCron();          // read from DB/config at runtime
    return new CronTrigger(cron).nextExecution(triggerContext);
});
```

Senior framing: `@Scheduled` is deliberately simple and perfect for fixed, in-process, best-effort periodic work. The moment I need concurrency, durability (must-not-miss, history, misfire), or runtime control, I reach for the appropriate escalation — a thread pool for concurrency, Quartz (or an external scheduler) for persistence and misfire, and programmatic/dynamic triggers or Quartz for runtime rescheduling. Combined with the earlier run-once concern in a cluster (ShedLock/leader/Quartz clustering), these decisions define a production scheduling strategy. I also always make jobs idempotent and observable, because any scheduler can double-fire or miss under edge conditions.

